          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em quaternion
            or
            %em Hamilton number
            is a kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number"} number
            similar to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            but with three instead of one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square+root"} square root
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1)
            adjoined, satisfying certain relations.
          %p
            The
            %strong quaternions
            form the largest
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+division+algebra"} normed division algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℍ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}
            after
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Rowan+Hamilton"} William Rowan Hamilton
            (since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            is taken for the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#normed_division_algebra_structure Normed division algebra structure
          %p
            The structure of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℍ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}
            as an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+unital+algebra"} algebra
            is given by a basis
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi j
                  %mo ,
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{1, i, j, k\}
            of the underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℍ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+element"} identity element
            and otherwise uniquely specified by the equations
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi i
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi j
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi k
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mi i
                  %mi j
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i^2 = j^2 = k^2 = i j k = -1,
          %p
            and extended by
            = succeed "-linearity" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℍ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℍ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}
            is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mi α
                  %mover
                    %mi α
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|\alpha\|}^2 = \alpha \widebar{\alpha}
          %p
            where given an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+combination"} linear combination
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi b
                    %mi i
                    %mo +
                    %mi c
                    %mi j
                    %mo +
                    %mi d
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha = a 1 + b i + c j + d k
            %em conjugate
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi α
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mi b
                    %mi i
                    %mo −
                    %mi c
                    %mi j
                    %mo −
                    %mi d
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widebar{\alpha} \coloneqq a 1 - b i - c j - d k
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi b
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi c
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi d
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|\alpha\|}^2 = a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2
          %p
            whence for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \neq 0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi α
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                          %mi α
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mn 2
                  %mover
                    %mi α
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha^{-1} = \frac1{{\|\alpha\|}^2} \widebar{\alpha}.
          %p
            In this way
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℍ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}
            is a normed division algebra.
          %h3#modules_and_bimodules Modules and bimodules
          %p
            We have canonical left and right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            structures on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℍ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℍ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}
            is not commutative, if we want to talk about tensor products of modules, we need to consider
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodules"} bimodules
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+algebra"} linear algebra
            as is used over a field is not quite the same when dealing with quaternions. For instance, one needs to distinguish between
            %em left
            and
            %em right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eigenvalues"} eigenvalues
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrices"} matrices
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℍ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_n(\mathbb{H})
            (using the left and right module structures on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℍ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}^n
            respectively), and only left eigenvalues relate to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            of the associated linear operator.
          %p
            Using the conjugation operation one can define an inner product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi q
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mover
                    %mi q
                    %mo ¯
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle q,p\rangle := \overline{q} p
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℍ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}^n
            so that the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+group+of+an+inner+product+space"} orthogonal group
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+symplectic+group"} compact symplectic group
          %h3#automorphisms Automorphisms
          #AutomorphismsOfQUatrnionsAlgebraIsSO3.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+group"} automorphism group
              of the quaternions, as a real algebra, is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+orthogonal+group"} SO(3)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} acting
              canonically on their imaginary part (in generalization of how the product of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
              respects the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+conjugation"} complex conjugation
              action)
          %p
            See also at
            %em
              %a{:href => "normed+division+algebra#Automorphisms"} normed division algebra – automorphism
          %p
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#KlimovZhuravlev"} Klimov-Zhuravlev, p. 85
